WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.029
The Joker is one of pop culture's most iconic
villains, and many actors have gotten the

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:06.360
chance to take on the role.

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:10.240
We've stacked the Jokers against each other
before, but there have been a few groundbreaking

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.640
interpretations of the character since then
that we have to talk about.

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:18.260
Keep watching to see how Joaquin Phoenix holds
up against Heath Ledger, Jack Nicholson, and

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.260
the rest, as we rank the Jokers, worst to
best.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.280
Despite coming in dead last on our list, the
version of the Joker that appeared on The

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:31.520
New Scooby-Doo Movies isn't unforgivably terrible,
he's just flat out boring, which might be

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:32.720
an even bigger problem.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.711
Plus, he's a complete failure, as he can't
manage to even scare Scooby-Doo, a character

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:40.220
defined by being terrified of everything.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:46.580
"Now, Pengy-Wengy, watch me lure them into
the room of doom."

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.140
The only thing that's really worth mentioning
about him is that he was voiced by veteran

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:55.940
comedian and actor Larry Storch, and let's
be real here: that's only really interesting

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.030
if you're the kind of person who likes to
get into some hardcore trivia about the cast

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:00.840
of F-Troop.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.780
"You've got to be putting me on!"

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.450
If you ever want to develop a whole new appreciation
for Batman: The Animated Series, take some

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:12.859
time to head back to 1977 for The New Batman
Adventures, which features some of the worst

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.320
character redesigns ever.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:19.500
Amazingly, the Joker, who was voiced by Lennie
Weinrib, managed to escape that particular

00:01:19.509 --> 00:01:24.060
flaw in the show, but "he looks a lot like
he does in the comics" is about the only good

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.280
thing you can say about him.

00:01:25.649 --> 00:01:30.939
His major accomplishment during the show's
entire 16-episode run was losing an election

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:35.100
for President of Criminals when the Penguin
invented a mind-altering substance called

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:35.980
"crime slime."

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.520
If you can't win an election against the Penguin,
what are you even doing?

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:45.600
"I, the notorious Joker, will stage the biggest
ripoff in the history of Gotham City."

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:51.479
In all honesty, we only included Dee Bradley
Baker's turn as the Joker on this list in

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.240
order to be as thorough as we can.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.799
That's not to knock the guy, but as the Joker's
appearance in Son of Batman is limited to

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.740
appearing as a shadow on a wall and letting
out one laugh.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.060
But…it was a nice laugh at least?

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.700
The Super Friends saga ran for eight years
under various titles, but the Joker only ever

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:19.020
appeared once, in 1985's "The Wild Cards,"
where he was voiced by Frank Welker.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:24.040
Unfortunately, his appearance was both minor
and bizarre, with the Joker turning up as

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:27.740
part of the playing card themed super villain
team the Royal Flush Gang.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:31.640
He's not the team's Joker, though, he's actually
disguised as the Ace.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.140
If your story calls for a chalk-white villain
named after a playing card to dress up as

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:40.420
a different chalk-white villain named after
a playing card, things are getting a little

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:41.900
needlessly complicated.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:47.870
"The Superpowers own headquarters will soon
be our new base on earth.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.640
Don't you love the irony?"

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.320
You know those memes where they intentionally
mix a bunch of geeky franchises in order to

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:56.640
induce nerd rage?

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.420
Well, the Young Justice version of the Joker
feels a lot like that.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:07.860
"Ladies and gentlemen, we interrupt your regularly
scheduled mayhem to bring you this.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:08.820
Important announcement.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:14.000
He's the Joker, but he looks like David Tennant
as the Doctor and is voiced by Brent Spiner

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.860
from Star Trek: The Next Generation.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.720
It's not exactly terrible, but it's overshadowed
by just about every other version of Joker

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:21.220
ever.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.740
Here's the weird thing about Steve Blum's
performance as the Joker: it's really only

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.040
here on a technicality.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.610
That's not a knock against Blum as an actor,
but rather has a lot to do with how those

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.010
games used to be structured.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:35.671
See, the original gag with the storylines
of the LEGO games is that since they were

00:03:35.671 --> 00:03:40.030
adapting incredibly popular franchises, the
developers figured everyone playing the games

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:41.190
already knew the story.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:44.840
That gave them the freedom to present their
versions as a slapstick pantomime, with all

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:49.380
the dialogue that players already knew by
heart replaced with incomprehensible muttering

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.220
and the occasional wordless reaction.

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:58.100
LEGO Batman, on the other hand, was the first
time the franchise had dipped its toe into

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:01.500
an original story, but they kept the pantomime
stylings for the first outing.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.240
As a result, Blum lent his voice to both Batman
and the Joker, but didn't wind up doing much

00:04:06.249 --> 00:04:09.819
more than a few grunts and a peal or two of
maniacal laughter in the role.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:15.290
To say the role of the Joker in an animated
version of Frank Miller's classic The Dark

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:19.100
Knight Returns presented a challenge is putting
things pretty mildly.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:24.580
The character's arc went from catatonic to
insanely murderous, requiring a ton of range.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.100
Person of Interest star Michael Emerson gave
it a shot, but played it just a little too

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:32.040
flat during the first parts of the Joker's
journey.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.730
At the end, he ramps up to a satisfying fever
pitch, but this one is a little too uneven

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:37.780
to rank any higher.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.220
"Doesn't matter.

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:40.440
I win.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.540
I made you lose control.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.480
And they'll kill you for it."

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.960
If you enjoyed the big-screen LEGO Batman
movie, then we have good news and bad news.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:59.940
The good news is that there's already a second
LEGO Batman movie you can watch, and in fact,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.880
it's been available on home video since 2013!

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:07.280
But here's the bad news: it's actually just
an extended adaptation of the storyline from

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:08.940
the LEGO Batman 2 video game.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:12.000
Really, though, that's only bad news if you've
already played the game.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:16.410
If you haven't, the actual storyline is really
fun, with the Joker and Lex Luthor teaming

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:20.750
up to terrorize Gotham City with a laser beam
that can deconstruct anything made of black

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:23.620
LEGO bricks, like, say, everything that Batman
owns.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.530
Christopher Corey Smith, who also played the
Joker in the second and third LEGO Batman

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:31.620
video games once they'd moved to a fully-voiced
story mode, is really just turning in a pretty

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.480
standard riff on Mark Hamill's Joker.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.980
It's perfectly good and highly enjoyable,
but it's not quite the original.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.400
"Well well.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.760
Lex Luthor.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:44.920
Presidential candidate and Superman’s least-favorite
bald person."

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:50.880
When you're hiring someone to play the Joker,
it stands to reason that two of the most important

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:55.760
qualifications are going to be a morbid sense
of humor and an insanely creepy laugh.

00:05:56.090 --> 00:06:00.120
If that's the case, you could do a hell of
a lot worse than just going out and getting

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:03.440
the guy who played the Cryptkeeper on Tales
From the Crypt.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.880
"Beware of skeletons.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.280
Unless they're yours truly."

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:15.220
That's exactly what happened in 2010, when
John Kassir lent his voice to a series of

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:18.640
shorts packaged with Fisher-Price's line of
Super Friends toys.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:23.940
Kassir does a good job of mixing up his performance,
though it's still instantly recognizable to

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:27.060
anyone who was a fan of the 80's HBO anthology.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:30.320
Which probably did not include any of the
kids in the target audience.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:31.120
We hope.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:32.300
"Finally!

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:33.800
You made it!

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.880
Now the fun can really begin!"

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:44.020
The Joker we're given in DC Super Friends
is pretty great if only because of how much

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:46.640
he clearly hates working with the other supervillains.

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:51.340
Voice actor Lloyd Floyd's audible eye rolling
adds some fun nuance to a performance that's

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:52.880
otherwise pretty much by the book.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:57.540
But hey: he does get bonus points for not
copying Mark Hamill like so many others have

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:58.040
done.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.580
"You created him Lexy?"

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:01.220
"Oh.

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:04.790
I thought you had no sense of humor, you old
card, you."

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:06.500
"It was a lab accident."

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.091
First, you have get past a design that starts
with a tattoo of the word "damaged" on his

00:07:11.091 --> 00:07:16.180
forehead and just spirals out from there until
he looks like he should be performing alongside

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:19.310
Dark Lotus at this year's Gathering of the
Juggalos.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:23.900
Then, you have to get past all the stories
of Jared Leto going Method and sending his

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:28.000
co-stars live rats, dead pigs, and used condoms.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.620
What you're left with is…well, not much
of anything, really.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.150
For all the hype surrounding Leto's appearance
as the Joker in Suicide Squad, it pretty much

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:49.120
amounted to about ten minutes of screentime
that were mostly there for Harley Quinn's

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:52.060
origin story and some ill-advised fanservice.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:57.360
When Arkham City was announced as Mark Hamill's
final outing as the Joker, there was a pretty

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.200
big problem.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:02.750
As Batman's arch-nemesis, the Clown Prince
of Crime was definitely going to be in the

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:07.610
next game, which told the story of an encounter
much earlier in Batman's career.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:12.681
Thus, the role of the Joker fell to Troy Baker,
and the problem here is obvious: he pretty

00:08:12.681 --> 00:08:16.320
much just did a dead-on impression of Hamill's
Joker for the entire game.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.890
To be fair, he actually does a really good
job of it, but a copy is just never going

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:21.840
to beat the original.

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:23.660
"Oh, Bats.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:30.580
What a night!"

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:35.190
The people behind Gotham clearly know that
they can't really have the Joker show up years

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:40.020
before Bruce Wayne becomes Batman on account
of his origin story being so tightly intertwined,

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:45.270
so they just went ahead and created a guy
who isn't technically the Joker, but is definitely

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:50.430
a maniacal supervillain with a permanent rictus
grin who dresses as a circus clown and wants

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:51.960
to sow chaos wherever he can.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:57.240
Cameron Monaghan's performance as Jerome Valeska
is ridiculously compelling, and has made for

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:58.900
some pretty wild television.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:01.400
"I'm the boss."

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.820
First things first: the Arkham franchise has
produced some of the best video games in recent

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:13.960
memory, and without question the best Batman
games ever.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:18.440
Unfortunately, even though they got the legendary
Mark Hamill to do the voice for three out

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:23.400
of four, they also ended up giving us a Joker
who has what might be the single stupidest

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.300
master plan in the character's 75-year history.

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:32.490
Seriously, after perfectly executing his scheme,
he unveils his ultimate masterstroke, which

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:37.260
is…turning himself into a giant drug monster
and getting in a fistfight with the greatest

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:38.940
hand to hand fighter in the world.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:40.040
Really, dude?

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:42.830
Dumb things like this are why you never beat
Batman!

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:44.260
"I can take it.

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:46.800
I can take anything you throw at me, Bats.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:49.760
You can't beat me.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:51.600
I'm actually going to win!"

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:56.880
The redesign of the Joker for The Batman is
remembered as one of the most divisive missteps

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:58.680
in the history of DC animation.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:03.630
Laboring under the shadow of the legendary
Batman: The Animated Series, and stuck with

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:08.510
the task of incorporating the "Batwave" gimmick
of the accompanying toy line, designer Jeff

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:13.650
Matsuda decided to go as far in the opposite
direction as possible from Bruce Timm's sleek

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:16.000
design from Batman: The Animated Series.

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:21.180
The result wasn't very well received, to put
it mildly, but Kevin Michael Richardson's

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:26.210
take on the Joker actually had some really
good stories, such as "The Laughing Bat,"

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:31.090
where Joker becomes a vigilante and uses Joke
Venom to turn Batman into a bad guy so he

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:32.420
has a supervillain to fight.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:33.700
That's pretty awesome.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:34.860
"Graffiti.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:38.440
That's a pretty serious offense, girls.

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:40.760
But that's why I became a crimefighter.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:43.400
To take out garbage like you."

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:47.200
Sometimes the Joker is a sinister, psychopathic
murderer.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:52.730
And then other times, he's a goofy and delightfully
manipulative weirdo who brings down an entire

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:54.920
city armed only with a spoon.

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:59.560
Joker stories that are actually funny are
pretty rare these days, so having a version

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:04.020
of The Joker as whimsical as Jason Spisak's
take on the character is a rare treat.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:09.180
And the fact that this short also introduced
his new sidekick Spoony is a hilarious bonus.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.640
"Can you make dogs invisible?"

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:14.080
"Spoony, that's not even a real super power."

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:17.380
"Perhaps a demonstration for Spoony?"

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:24.340
Adam West, Burt Ward, and Julie Newmar returned
to the roles of Batman, Robin, and Catwoman

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.570
in Batman: Return of the Caped Crusaders.

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:31.540
That was great, but the rest of the cast was
given the unenviable task of playing specific

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:35.860
versions of characters identified with actors
who had died years before.

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.800
For Jeff Bergman, that meant playing Cesar
Romero playing the Joker.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:45.010
It was a high wire act, as Bergman had to
be respectful of both Romero and Romero's

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:50.360
take on the Joker, but Bergman nailed it in
a performance that feels like a true tribute.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:52.180
"You dare defy us?

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:53.820
You must be insane.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:55.480
And here's the proof."

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:03.220
From day one, Gotham has been a show that
wanted to have its cake and eat it too, and

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:06.480
there's no character who embodies that spirit
more than Jeremiah Valeska.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.880
"I wanna be the star of the show!"

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:13.960
Gotham spent years being a Batman show without
Batman, where the Riddler wasn't the Riddler

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.140
yet and the Penguin wasn't the Penguin yet.

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:20.370
But all of the weirdest elements of the franchise,
like the Order of St. Dumas and Professor

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.220
Pyg, are present and accounted for.

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:26.950
In this context, Jeremiah is the closest we
got to a proper Joker, and he still doesn't

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:27.740
quite make the cut.

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:31.600
Not only is he a double fake out, the twin
brother of the guy that we thought was going

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:35.630
to be the Joker before he died, who then got
dunked in chemicals and took a liking to purple

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:40.390
suits, he also comes as close as you can possibly
get to being the Joker without actually stepping

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:41.440
across the finish line.

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:46.000
Even in the series finale, he steadfastly
refuses to pay off the setup, instead referring

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.340
to himself by every J-name in the book except
the one we want.

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:52.160
"I dunno, call me Jack.

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:54.100
No, that's not right.

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:54.740
Joseph.

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:55.640
John.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:57.000
J. I don't know."

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:00.720
And as frustrating as it is, that's also kind
of great.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:04.379
There's a deliberate goofiness to the way
he dances around "the Joker issue" that's

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:05.560
genuinely charming and fun.

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:09.160
He's the kind of character that makes you
wish the entire series had been this bonkers

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:10.160
from the beginning.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:15.340
Another show that had to follow in the footsteps
of Batman: The Animated Series was Batman:

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:20.420
The Brave and the Bold, which decided to embrace
the character's lighter Silver Age phase from

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:22.580
the comics of 50's and 60's.

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:27.560
This Joker, which was voiced by Jeff Bennett,
looked a lot like the work of legendary Batman

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:32.940
artist Dick Sprang, and really shined in stories
like the alternate world tale of Earth-3,

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:37.930
where a heroic version of The Joker became
the last superhero on Earth under the name

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:38.920
The Red Hood.

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:43.600
"Whoever he is, I hope my counterpart on your
world will have a chance to repay you.

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:45.480
"Somehow, that seems unlikely."

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:50.280
You really have to give it to Joaquin Phoenix
for his performance in the title role of 2019's

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:52.100
Joker: he commits to the part.

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:54.420
Arthur Fleck is legitimately difficult to
watch.

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:59.040
There’s the grating of his compulsive laughter,
the body horror shots of his emaciated figure

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:02.360
and spindly limbs, and the disturbing relationship
with his mother.

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:06.850
We watch him go through a seemingly endless
string of physical and emotional beatdowns,

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:08.520
and Phoenix is nothing if not committed.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:13.399
His shift from the pathetic Fleck to the twisted
Joker is a masterful performance indeed.

00:14:13.399 --> 00:14:16.580
Unfortunately, that performance is in a movie
that doesn't deserve it.

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:20.330
No matter how compelling you find Phoenix's
performance, a lot of the dialogue will make

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:21.300
you roll your eyes.

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:25.100
The worst sin of Joker, though, is that it
strips all of the mystery away from the character.

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:29.600
That's what sets Joker apart from other supervillains
and makes him as compelling and scary as he

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:30.100
is.

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:34.880
With Arthur Fleck, we get a meticulously detailed
report about his childhood abuse, miserable

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:39.320
adulthood, and how he got his name, explained
a couple of times in case you missed it.

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:45.520
Jack Nicholson's performance as the Joker
in Tim Burton's 1989 Batman movie is pretty

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:47.320
fantastic on almost every level.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:52.520
He's certainly the best part of the movie,
with Nicholson's already-creepy grin accentuated

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:56.120
by caked on makeup and some truly amazing
fashion choices.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:01.080
And the scene where he trashes an art museum
might be the most baller thing a supervillain

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:01.880
has ever done.

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:03.740
"Gentlemen!

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:05.800
Let's broaden our minds.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:07.120
Lawrence!"

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:12.060
The only problem with this version of Joker
is his alter-ego, Jack Napier.

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:16.790
In the comics, a big part of Joker's origin
is the idea that some fundamental change in

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:19.899
his personality occurred when he fell in that
vat of acid.

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:22.500
Here, though, he's pretty much already The
Joker.

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:24.660
He even carries a deck of cards with him.

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:27.700
And he's obviously a criminal, and that undermines
his arc.

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:29.660
Otherwise, though, it's totally awesome.

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:35.260
Even if you don't recognize his name, you're
almost certainly familiar with John DiMaggio

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:40.430
from his roles as Bender on Futurama, Jake
the Dog on Adventure Time, and Aquaman on

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:41.660
Batman: The Brave and the Bold.

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:46.880
In 2010, he landed the role of the Joker in
Under the Red Hood, and he didn't disappoint,

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:51.420
delivering a Joker who delivers every line
as though it is an actual joke, giving you

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:55.600
the sense of someone who genuinely thinks
that beating someone to death with a crowbar

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:56.540
is hilarious.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:59.080
It's memorable in the creepiest of ways.

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:02.380
"So…let's try and clear this up, okay pumpkin?"

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:08.040
Pretty much everything about Cesar Romero's
portrayal of the Joker on the 1966 Batman

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:13.459
TV show is great, from the way he attacks
every scene with manic, scenery-chewing glee

00:16:13.459 --> 00:16:18.330
to the way he twists his painted-on grin into
a disappointed scowl when he's inevitably

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:18.840
defeated.

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:24.220
All the way down to the fact that Romero refused
to shave his mustache for the part, instead

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:28.240
caking on the clown makeup and leaving it
completely visible in every episode.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:32.220
There's a panache and even a little menace
to the role that makes him one of the show's

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:33.960
most memorable characters.

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:38.620
"How delicious it is?"

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:44.080
Unfortunately, the show's writers were bigger
fans of The Penguin and Catwoman, and Romero

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:49.100
often got stuck in boring and forgettable
stories which didn't allow him to really shine.

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:50.380
A true shame.

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:55.660
Of all the Joker's appearances across movies
and TV, who would've expected that the one

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:59.280
that really went into whether his motivation
stemmed from a twisted sort of love would

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:02.260
be the one based on building block toys for
tiny children?

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:07.000
And yet here we are, in a world where that's
just not acknowledged in the LEGO Batman Movie,

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.840
but serves as the driving force for the entire
plot.

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:15.680
Zack Galifianakis nails the voicework too,
making this modern Joker one of the best ever.

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:17.280
"You're obsessed with me!"

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:18.260
"Phhhhhhh.

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:18.760
No I'm not.

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:19.780
"Yes you are."

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:20.280
"No, I'm not."

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:20.780
"YES YOU ARE.

00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:23.220
Who else drives you to one-up them the way
that I do?"

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:23.720
"Bane."

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:24.880
"No he doesn't!"

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:30.220
Between the massive initial hype and the outpouring
of grief following the untimely death of actor

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:34.789
Heath Ledger, it can be a little difficult
to judge his performance as The Joker on its

00:17:34.789 --> 00:17:35.600
own merits.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:40.840
But when you put aside all the baggage and
really look at it, the truth becomes clear:

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:42.180
it really is that good.

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:44.560
"The good cop bad cop routine?"

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:46.400
"Not exactly."

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:58.720
The Joker of The Dark Knight is both terrifying
and genuinely funny, but more than that, he's

00:17:58.721 --> 00:18:04.059
got an air of mystery that's almost impossible
for a character so well-known to cultivate,

00:18:04.059 --> 00:18:09.049
with virtually every line he delivers turning
out to be a carefully conceived, manipulative

00:18:09.049 --> 00:18:09.540
lie.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:14.780
Here's one truth you can bank on though: Ledger's
Joker is one of the most influential and iconic

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:16.240
movie villains of all time.

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:19.080
But he's still not the best Joker of all time…

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:24.580
When you get right down to it, Batman: The
Animated Series did everything right.

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:29.820
The slick, stylish take on the Caped Crusader
boiled everything down to essentials, and

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:32.309
no character benefitted as much as the Joker.

00:18:32.309 --> 00:18:37.230
This Joker was frightening and funny, with
a sweeping theatricality that came directly

00:18:37.230 --> 00:18:40.080
from Mark Hamill's amazing turn providing
his voice.

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:49.749
And the desire to give Hamill's Joker even
more to do directly led to the creation of

00:18:49.749 --> 00:18:54.160
Harley Quinn, who has gone on to become one
of DC's most popular characters.

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:58.840
Add it all up and the Animated Series Joker
remains the definitive take on the character.

00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:00.960
And that's…no joke.

